<article class="cfg-article">

<div class="cfg-article__grid">
<div class="cfg-article__main">

    <nav class="cfg-article__crumbs">
        <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
                    <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/category/ai/">AI</a>
            <span>/</span>
                <span>Ollama Models Cheat Sheet 2026 (gpt-oss, Qwen3-Coder, DeepSeek)</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="cfg-article__header">
                    <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/category/ai/" class="cfg-article__cat">AI</a>
                <h1 class="cfg-article__title">Ollama Models Cheat Sheet 2026 (gpt-oss, Qwen3-Coder, DeepSeek)</h1>
        <div class="cfg-article__meta">
            <img src="https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/6074c6c139bb83d4ccb6f0bbebbe5194b528c07121ca3022ad741d8f2d1c5bdf?s=40&#038;r=g" alt="" class="cfg-article__avatar">
            <div>
                <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/author/mutai-josphat/" class="cfg-article__author">Josphat Mutai</a>
                <span class="cfg-article__date">Updated Sep 25, 2026 &middot; 34 min read</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="entry-content cfg-article__body">
        
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ollama makes running open-weight LLMs locally as easy as <code>ollama run</code>, but the hard part is picking <em>which</em> model to run. Which hardware runs it is the other half of the question: a <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/ryzen-ai-max-395-mini-pc-comparison/">128GB Ryzen AI Max+ mini PC</a> can hold models a graphics card cannot, and <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/unified-memory-vs-vram-for-ai/">unified memory vs VRAM</a> explains the trade-off. The library now ships hundreds of variants across gpt-oss, Llama, Mistral, Gemma 4, DeepSeek, the Qwen 3.5, 3.6, and 3.8 generation, qwen3-coder, IBM Granite, Meta&#8217;s Muse Glimmer, NVIDIA Nemotron, Liquid LFM, Phi, and a steadily growing list of embedding and vision models, each with its own size tags, context window, default quantization, and tradeoffs. This cheat sheet is a model-selection guide, not a CLI reference. It tells you what to pull for the job at hand, how much VRAM each pick really needs, what quantization actually means in practice, and how the popular models compare on the same benchmark prompts. Every model in the benchmark table was pulled and rerun on Ollama 0.34.4 with an NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24 GB VRAM, driver 580.119.02) under Ubuntu 24.04 in September 2026.</p><span data-cfg="822aa603a34f" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;font-size:0;line-height:0;overflow:hidden;">Original content from computingforgeeks.com - post 167394</span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you came here looking for command syntax (<code>pull</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>ps</code>, <code>show</code>, environment variables, REST API), keep that window open at the <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/ollama-commands-cheat-sheet/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Ollama commands cheat sheet</a>. This piece picks up where that one stops, at the question every reader sends afterwards: which model do I actually run?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The September rerun turned up one change that matters more than any new model. The current Qwen 3.6, Qwen 3.8, and Gemma 4 26B builds ship with speculative decoding switched on in their default parameters, and on the same card that roughly doubles generation speed over the July numbers (<code>qwen3.6:27b</code> went from 48 to 115 tok/s, <code>gemma4:26b</code> from 157 to 294). The <a href="#benchmark-results">benchmark section</a> has the before and after.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="701" src="https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ollama-list-rtx4090-2026-09-1024x701.png" alt="ollama list output showing 31 pulled models on the RTX 4090 test rig" class="wp-image-171940" title="Ollama Models Cheat Sheet 2026 (gpt-oss, Qwen3-Coder, DeepSeek) 1" srcset="https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ollama-list-rtx4090-2026-09-1024x701.png 1024w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ollama-list-rtx4090-2026-09-300x205.png 300w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ollama-list-rtx4090-2026-09-768x526.png 768w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ollama-list-rtx4090-2026-09-1536x1051.png 1536w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ollama-list-rtx4090-2026-09-2048x1402.png 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"><code>ollama list</code> on the RTX 4090 test instance after pulling all 31 benchmarked models.</figcaption></figure>



<h2 id="the-60-second-decision-tree" class="wp-block-heading">The 60-second decision tree</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Read this first. The rest of the article justifies it.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>You have</th><th>Best general chat</th><th>Best coding</th><th>Best vision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>4 GB VRAM (or CPU only with 8 GB RAM)</td><td><code>llama3.2:1b</code> or <code>gemma3:1b</code></td><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:1.5b</code></td><td><code>llava:7b</code> on CPU (slow)</td></tr>
<tr><td>8 GB VRAM (RTX 3060, RTX 4060)</td><td><code>qwen3.5:9b</code>, <code>granite4.2:8b</code>, <code>lfm2.5</code>, <code>llama3.1:8b</code></td><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:7b</code></td><td><code>qwen3.5:9b</code> or <code>llava:7b</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>12 GB VRAM (RTX 3060 12GB, RTX 4070)</td><td><code>gemma4:12b</code>, <code>qwen3.5:9b</code>, <code>mistral-nemo:12b</code></td><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:14b</code> (Q4)</td><td><code>gemma4:12b</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>16 GB VRAM (RTX 4080, RTX 5060 Ti 16GB)</td><td><code>gpt-oss:20b</code>, <code>gemma4:12b</code>, <code>qwen3.5:9b</code></td><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:14b</code> (Q5)</td><td><code>gemma4:12b</code>, <code>qwen3-vl:8b</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>24 GB VRAM (RTX 3090, RTX 4090, RTX 5090 budget)</td><td><code>qwen3.8:27b</code>, <code>qwen3.6:35b</code>, <code>gpt-oss:20b</code>, <code>gemma4:26b</code></td><td><code>qwen3-coder:30b</code>, <code>qwen3.8:27b</code></td><td><code>qwen3.8:27b</code>, <code>gemma4:26b</code>, <code>muse-glimmer:30b</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>48 GB+ VRAM (2x RTX 3090, RTX 6000 Ada, A6000)</td><td><code>gpt-oss:120b</code> (80 GB), <code>mistral-medium-3.5:128b</code> (96 GB+), <code>llama3.3:70b</code></td><td><code>qwen3-coder:30b</code> (Q8), <code>deepseek-coder-v2:16b</code></td><td><code>qwen3-vl:32b</code>, <code>llama3.2-vision:90b</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you&#8217;re on Apple Silicon, treat unified memory as VRAM and cut the table by ~30 percent for headroom (macOS reserves a chunk for the OS). An M2 Pro 32 GB behaves like a 24 GB GPU for Ollama, an M3 Max 64 GB behaves like a 48 GB GPU. A 128 GB Mac is the only single box that runs <code>qwen3.8-flash-next:125b-mlx</code>, a 105 GB MLX-only preview of Qwen&#8217;s next architecture. For picking an actual card, see which <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/best-gpu-for-llm/">GPU to buy for local LLMs</a>, ranked by VRAM and model size. If the memory ceiling matters more to you than raw speed, the <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/best-mini-pc-for-local-ai-llm/">mini PCs worth buying for local AI</a> take the unified-memory route instead.</p>



<h2 id="ollama-models-list-see-what-is-installed-and-what-is-new" class="wp-block-heading">Ollama models list: see what is installed and what is new</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To list the Ollama models on your machine, run <code>ollama list</code> (or its alias <code>ollama ls</code>). It prints every pulled model with its tag, ID, size on disk, and when it was pulled or updated:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code code"><code>ollama list</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This is the full list from the RTX 4090 test instance after pulling every model benchmarked below. The SIZE column is the download size, which is the floor for the memory the model needs once loaded:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code code"><code>NAME                             ID              SIZE      MODIFIED
nemotron-3.5-lightning:latest    e7a64ff15fb1    25 GB     27 minutes ago
muse-glimmer:30b                 de878ce33ad8    18 GB     29 minutes ago
granite4.2:30b                   be22829aa92a    17 GB     30 minutes ago
qwen3.6:35b                      096fdbd02fe6    22 GB     31 minutes ago
qwen3.8:27b                      aaee06c39dcf    17 GB     33 minutes ago
deepseek-r1:32b                  edba8017331d    19 GB     34 minutes ago
gemma3:27b                       a418f5838eaf    17 GB     35 minutes ago
qwen3.6:27b                      9d5803d493a9    17 GB     36 minutes ago
qwen3.5:27b                      7653528ba5cb    17 GB     37 minutes ago
gemma4:26b                       08ae7ec1744b    18 GB     38 minutes ago
qwen3-coder:30b                  06c1097efce0    18 GB     40 minutes ago
gpt-oss:20b                      17052f91a42e    13 GB     41 minutes ago
gemma4:12b                       4eb23ef187e2    7.6 GB    41 minutes ago
gemma3:12b                       f4031aab637d    8.1 GB    42 minutes ago
lfm2.5:latest                    9cf756159fc2    5.2 GB    42 minutes ago
granite4.2:8b                    f586c02fdecd    5.3 GB    42 minutes ago
qwen3.5:9b                       6488c96fa5fa    6.6 GB    42 minutes ago
qwen3-vl:8b                      901cae732162    6.1 GB    43 minutes ago
deepseek-r1:8b                   6995872bfe4c    5.2 GB    43 minutes ago
qwen3:8b                         500a1f067a9f    5.2 GB    43 minutes ago
llama3.1:8b                      46e0c10c039e    4.9 GB    43 minutes ago
qwen2.5:7b                       845dbda0ea48    4.7 GB    43 minutes ago
qwen2.5-coder:7b                 dae161e27b0e    4.7 GB    44 minutes ago
mistral:7b                       6577803aa9a0    4.4 GB    44 minutes ago
qwen3.5:2b                       324d162be6ca    2.7 GB    44 minutes ago
qwen3.5:4b                       2a654d98e6fb    3.4 GB    44 minutes ago
phi4-mini:latest                 78fad5d182a7    2.5 GB    44 minutes ago
llama3.2:3b                      a80c4f17acd5    2.0 GB    45 minutes ago
qwen3.5:0.8b                     f3817196d142    1.0 GB    45 minutes ago
deepseek-r1:1.5b                 e0979632db5a    1.1 GB    45 minutes ago
llama3.2:1b                      baf6a787fdff    1.3 GB    45 minutes ago</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Scripts and dashboards should read the same list from the REST API instead of parsing the table. The <code>/api/tags</code> endpoint returns JSON with the name, size in bytes, digest, and quantization of every local model:</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code code"><code>curl -s http://localhost:11434/api/tags | jq -r '.models[] | "\(.name)\t\(.size)"'</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For models that are loaded in memory right now, <code>ollama ps</code> is the command, and its PROCESSOR column tells you whether a model sits fully on the GPU. For the full catalogue of models you can pull, browse <a href="https://ollama.com/search" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">ollama.com/search</a>, which sorts by popularity or newest.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">New local models worth pulling</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These are the additions worth putting on the shortlist, most of them released since the summer. Sizes are the default tag as <code>ollama list</code> or the library page reports it, and the context is the trained maximum from the model page:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Pull</th><th>Size</th><th>Context</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.8:27b</code></td><td>17 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>Newest dense Qwen. Vision, tools, and thinking with low / medium / xhigh effort levels. Speculative decoding on by default</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.6:35b</code></td><td>22 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>35B mixture-of-experts sibling of the 27b. The fastest big model on a 24 GB card in our run</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>muse-glimmer:30b</code></td><td>18 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>Meta&#8217;s 30B agentic model with a vision encoder, distilled from Muse Spark</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nemotron-3.5-lightning</code></td><td>25 GB</td><td>1M</td><td>NVIDIA 30B MoE, 3B active, built for always-on agents. Does not fit a 24 GB card whole</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>granite4.2</code></td><td>2.2 / 5.3 / 17 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>IBM&#8217;s dense 3b, 8b, and 30b. Apache 2.0, tools, JSON output, thinking mode</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lfm2.5</code></td><td>5.2 GB</td><td>125K</td><td>Liquid AI&#8217;s 8B hybrid for on-device agents. Generates at small-model speed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mistral-medium-3.5:128b</code></td><td>80 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>Mistral&#8217;s dense 128B flagship with vision and per-request reasoning effort. Needs 96 GB or more of VRAM</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.8-flash-next:125b-mlx</code></td><td>105 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>125B MoE, 6B active, the first open preview of the Qwen4 architecture. MLX only, so Apple Silicon only</td></tr>
</tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Several of the biggest 2026 releases never get a local tag at all. <code>deepseek-v4-pro</code>, <code>deepseek-v4.1-flash</code>, <code>kimi-k3</code>, <code>glm-5.3</code>, <code>glm-5.3-flash</code>, and <code>minimax-m3</code> are published as <code>:cloud</code> tags only, so <code>ollama run kimi-k3:cloud</code> works while a plain <code>ollama pull kimi-k3</code> has nothing local to fetch. Running Kimi K3 on your own hardware means the GGUF route in <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/run-kimi-k3-locally/">how to run Kimi K3 locally</a>, and the cloud pricing is further down this page.</p>



<h2 id="install-ollama-in-30-seconds" class="wp-block-heading">Install Ollama in 30 seconds</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The same one-liner works on every supported Linux. On macOS download the .dmg from <a href="https://ollama.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">ollama.com/download</a>; on Windows use the .exe installer.</p>



<pre class="wp-block-code code"><code>curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
ollama --version
# ollama version is 0.34.4</code></pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The installer detects your GPU, drops the binary into <code>/usr/local/bin/ollama</code>, creates an <code>ollama</code> system user, and registers a <code>systemd</code> unit at <code>/etc/systemd/system/ollama.service</code>. The API listens on <code>127.0.0.1:11434</code> after install. For the multi-OS walkthrough with SELinux, firewall, and Nginx reverse proxy, follow the <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/install-ollama-rocky-ubuntu/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Ollama install guide for Rocky Linux 10 / Ubuntu 24.04</a>. For the complete CLI, REST API, Python SDK, Modelfile, and environment variable reference, keep the <a href="https://computingforgeeks.com/ollama-commands-cheat-sheet/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Ollama commands cheat sheet</a> open in a separate tab; this article focuses on which model to run, why, and how it performs.</p>



<h2 id="the-ollama-model-library-in-2026-categorized" class="wp-block-heading">The Ollama model library in 2026, categorized</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="2560" height="1600" src="https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular.png" alt="ollama.com/library showing top models by pulls (llama3.1, deepseek-r1, nomic-embed-text)" class="wp-image-167390" title="Ollama Models Cheat Sheet 2026 (gpt-oss, Qwen3-Coder, DeepSeek) 2" srcset="https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular.png 2560w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular-300x188.png 300w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular-1024x640.png 1024w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular-768x480.png 768w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular-1536x960.png 1536w, https://computingforgeeks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ollama-library-popular-2048x1280.png 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">The Ollama library page sorts by popularity. The top three slots are usually llama3.1, deepseek-r1, and nomic-embed-text.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ollama&#8217;s library page lists hundreds of tags, but most fall into four pillars. Treat them separately. A coding model is not a chat model with extra steps, an embedding model is not a tiny chat model, a vision model is not a chat model with image upload bolted on. Choose the pillar first, then the size.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">General chat and reasoning models</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Sizes on Ollama</th><th>Default file</th><th>Ctx</th><th>Tools</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>gpt-oss</code></td><td>20b, 120b</td><td>14 / 65 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>OpenAI&#8217;s open-weight MoE (Apache 2.0). 20b runs in 16 GB, 120b on one 80 GB GPU. The strongest open general/reasoning pick for most 2026 rigs</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>llama3.3</code></td><td>70b</td><td>43 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Still a solid, widely supported 70B baseline; performs near Llama 3.1 405B. No longer the outright best open 70B (gpt-oss and Qwen3 caught up)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>llama3.2</code></td><td>1b, 3b</td><td>1.3 / 2.0 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>On-device, edge, mobile, fast tool routing, lightweight chat</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>llama3.1</code></td><td>8b, 70b, 405b</td><td>4.9 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Solid 8B baseline, the safe default; 405B for serious infrastructure</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>llama4</code></td><td>16x17b (Scout), 128x17b (Maverick)</td><td>67 / 245 GB</td><td>10M / 1M</td><td>yes</td><td>Natively multimodal MoE. Scout needs ~60 GB+ VRAM, Maverick is multi-GPU/enterprise only</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mistral-nemo</code></td><td>12b</td><td>7.1 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Drop-in upgrade from Mistral 7B; multilingual; the right &#8220;step up from 8B&#8221; pick</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mistral-small</code></td><td>22b, 24b</td><td>14 GB</td><td>32K to 128K</td><td>yes (native)</td><td>Agentic workloads, JSON output, function calling, the strongest mid-range chat model</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mistral</code></td><td>7b</td><td>4.1 GB</td><td>32K</td><td>partial</td><td>Apache 2.0 license requirement, legacy compatibility. New projects should pick Nemo</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mixtral</code></td><td>8x7b, 8x22b</td><td>26 / 80 GB</td><td>32K / 64K</td><td>yes</td><td>Quality per active parameter, but loads all experts in memory. Needs ≥48 GB</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>gemma4</code></td><td>e2b, e4b, 12b, 26b, 31b</td><td>7.6 / 17 / 20 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>yes (native)</td><td>The current Gemma. Every size is multimodal with tool-calling and thinking built in. The 12b (dense, 11.9B) fits 12 GB; the 26b is a 26B-A4B MoE that runs at ~4B speed on a 24 GB card. Successor to gemma3</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>gemma3</code></td><td>270m, 1b, 4b, 12b, 27b</td><td>varies</td><td>32K (1B) / 128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Best multilingual coverage (140 languages); 4b and up include vision. Superseded by gemma4 for new work</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>deepseek-r1</code></td><td>1.5b, 7b, 8b, 14b, 32b, 70b, 671b</td><td>varies</td><td>128K / 160K</td><td>indirect</td><td>Reasoning, math, chain-of-thought workloads. Slower because the model thinks before answering (latest update is 0528)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>deepseek-v3.1</code></td><td>671b</td><td>404 GB</td><td>160K</td><td>yes</td><td>The last DeepSeek generation with local weights on Ollama (671B / ~37B active). Hybrid thinking and non-thinking. Serious multi-GPU territory</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>deepseek-v4-pro</code>, <code>deepseek-v4.1-flash</code></td><td>cloud only</td><td>n/a</td><td>varies</td><td>yes</td><td>The current DeepSeek models. On Ollama they exist only as <code>:cloud</code> tags</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>granite4.2</code></td><td>3b, 8b, 30b</td><td>2.2 / 5.3 / 17 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>IBM&#8217;s dense Apache 2.0 family with thinking mode and structured JSON output. Solid enterprise RAG pick</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lfm2.5</code></td><td>8b</td><td>5.2 GB</td><td>125K</td><td>yes</td><td>Liquid AI hybrid built for on-device agents. The fastest 5 GB-class model we have measured on Ollama</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>muse-glimmer</code></td><td>30b</td><td>18 GB</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Meta&#8217;s local agentic model with vision, distilled from Muse Spark</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nemotron-3.5-lightning</code></td><td>30b</td><td>25 GB</td><td>1M</td><td>yes</td><td>NVIDIA&#8217;s 3B-active MoE for always-on agents. Needs more than 24 GB to stay on the GPU</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mistral-medium-3.5</code></td><td>128b</td><td>80 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>yes</td><td>Dense 128B Mistral flagship with vision and configurable reasoning effort</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>phi4</code></td><td>14b</td><td>9.1 GB</td><td>16K</td><td>weak</td><td>Dense knowledge per parameter, STEM reasoning. Avoid for tool-using agents</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen2.5</code></td><td>0.5b, 1.5b, 3b, 7b, 14b, 32b, 72b</td><td>varies</td><td>up to 128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Best multilingual + reasoning under 32B, especially for Chinese, Japanese, Korean</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.8</code></td><td>27b</td><td>17 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>yes</td><td>The newest Qwen. Dense 27B with vision, tools, and adjustable thinking depth; fits a 24 GB card at Q4 and runs speculative decoding by default</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.6</code></td><td>27b, 35b</td><td>17 / 22 GB</td><td>256K</td><td>yes</td><td>Tuned for agentic coding and long-horizon reasoning. The 27b is dense and multimodal; the 35b is a mixture-of-experts that fits 24 GB at an 8K context and generates at 7B-class speed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.5</code></td><td>0.8b, 2b, 4b, 9b, 27b, 35b, 122b</td><td>varies</td><td>256K</td><td>yes</td><td>Hybrid Gated-Delta + sparse-MoE generation. Every size is multimodal with tools and thinking. The 9b fits 8 GB, the 27b fits 24 GB, the 122b is cloud/multi-GPU</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3</code></td><td>0.6b, 1.7b, 4b, 8b, 14b, 30b, 32b, 235b</td><td>varies</td><td>40K to 256K</td><td>yes</td><td>Still fine where you have tooling built around it. Dense up to 32b, MoE at 30b/235b. Superseded by qwen3.5, qwen3.6, and qwen3.8 for new work</td></tr>
</tbody></table></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Coding models</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Sizes</th><th>Ctx</th><th>FIM</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>qwen3-coder</code></td><td>30b, 480b</td><td>256K (to 1M)</td><td>yes</td><td>The new local-coding leader. 30b (19 GB) is a 30B-A3B MoE that fits a 24 GB card at Q4 and is the strongest model most people can run locally for agentic coding</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen2.5-coder</code></td><td>0.5b, 1.5b, 3b, 7b, 14b, 32b</td><td>32K</td><td>yes</td><td>The best small and mid-size coding family (0.5b to 32b). Reach for it when you cannot run qwen3-coder:30b; the 32B still scores near GPT-4o on Aider</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>deepseek-coder-v2</code></td><td>16b, 236b</td><td>16b: 160K, 236b: 4K</td><td>yes</td><td>Long-file refactors, repo-aware tasks; 16B is MoE so the active param count is small. The 236b on Ollama is capped at 4K context</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>codegemma</code></td><td>2b, 7b</td><td>8K</td><td>yes (2b code variant)</td><td>Lightweight inline completion in editor plugins</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>starcoder2</code></td><td>3b, 7b, 15b</td><td>16K</td><td>yes</td><td>Permissive license, 600+ languages on the 15B; reach for it when license matters</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>deepseek-coder</code></td><td>1.3b, 6.7b, 33b</td><td>16K</td><td>yes</td><td>Legacy projects and the 1.3B for edge devices. New work, pick V2 or qwen2.5-coder</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>devstral</code></td><td>24b</td><td>128K</td><td>yes</td><td>Local agentic coding from Mistral. Needs ≥16 GB VRAM</td></tr>
</tbody></table></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Vision (multimodal) models</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Sizes</th><th>Ctx</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>qwen3.8</code></td><td>27b</td><td>256K</td><td>Vision is built into the newest general Qwen, so one 17 GB pull covers chat, tools, and images on a 24 GB card</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>muse-glimmer</code></td><td>30b</td><td>128K</td><td>Meta&#8217;s agentic model with a dedicated perception encoder; built to act on what it sees</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qwen3-vl</code></td><td>2b, 4b, 8b, 30b, 32b, 235b</td><td>256K (to 1M)</td><td>The strongest open vision-language family on Ollama now. Best on screenshots, UI, and visual agents; the 8b fits 8 GB</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>gemma4</code></td><td>12b, 26b, 31b</td><td>256K</td><td>The current compact multimodal pick. Vision on every size plus tool-calling and thinking; the 12b fits 12 GB and the 26b is an A4B MoE that stays fast on a 24 GB card. Replaces gemma3 for new work</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>gemma3</code></td><td>4b, 12b, 27b</td><td>128K</td><td>Still strong on document, chart, and OCR work (DocVQA 85.6 on the 27B per Google&#8217;s report). Superseded by gemma4</td></tr>
